- internal/cache/cache.go            — cache.Store wraps redis.Client with in-memory fallback; has IsRedis() but no Underlying()
- internal/platform/cache/connect.go — platform cache.Client with Underlying() *redis.Client
- internal/api/telemetry_handler.go  — TelemetryHandler struct; signalStore.Update at line ~464; safeGo pattern for async work
- cmd/teslasync/main.go              — cacheStore := cache.New(cfg.Redis); telemetryHandler.SetSignalStore(signalStore)

Key findings:
1. redis.Client is github.com/redis/go-redis/v9 (v9.18.0)
2. signalStore.Update is called at line 464 (after VIN→vehicleID lookup)
3. Existing pattern: safeGo("name", func(){}) for fire-and-forget async with panic recovery
4. Setter pattern: SetSignalStore, SetEventHub, SetRedisCache — called from main.go post-construction
5. Signal values in map are: float64, int, int64, bool, string, map[string]interface{} ({invalid:true} markers)
6. internal/cache.Store.rdb is unexported — new RedisSignalCache takes *redis.Client directly

=== REASONING ===

Design decisions:
1. RedisSignalCache takes *redis.Client (not cache.Store) — keeps signal package independent of cache package
2. HSET variadic for single round-trip per batch — matches task requirement
3. 7-day TTL via EXPIRE after HSET — auto-cleans stale vehicles
4. Value encoding: fmt-based string conversion (no JSON wrapping) — keeps values human-readable in redis-cli
5. Skip nil values and {invalid:true} markers — mirrors in-memory Store.Update filtering
6. Fire-and-forget via safeGo (not raw goroutine) — catches panics, follows codebase pattern
7. Map copy before goroutine — prevents concurrent map read/write (same pattern as MongoDB signal log)
8. SetRedisCache setter — follows established pattern (SetSignalStore, SetEventHub)

Redis key schema: "vehicle:{vehicleID}:signals" (no teslasync: prefix — this is signal-specific, not general cache)

